changeSet.sql:
BEGIN
    DBMS_STATS.gather_table_stats(
        ownname => USER,
        tabname => 'SENDER_STAGE',
        cascade => TRUE,
        estimate_percent => 100
    );
END;
/

changeSet.comment:
Performance Maintenance Guide for SENDER_STAGE Indexes:
Weekly Maintenance: ================== 1. Update index statistics: Oracle: EXEC DBMS_STATS.gather_table_stats(USER, 'SENDER_STAGE', cascade => TRUE); H2: ANALYZE TABLE SENDER_STAGE; PostgreSQL: ANALYZE SENDER_STAGE;
2. Monitor index fragmentation: Oracle: SELECT index_name, blevel, del_lf_rows FROM user_indexes WHERE table_name = 'SENDER_STAGE';
Monthly Maintenance: ==================== 1. Rebuild fragmented indexes (if fragmentation > 30%): Oracle: ALTER INDEX idx_sender_stage_request_status REBUILD;
2. Check unused indexes: Oracle: SELECT * FROM v$object_usage WHERE object_name LIKE 'idx_sender_stage%';
Performance Targets: ==================== Aggregation queries (100k records): < 500ms (with indexes < 150ms) Timeout detection (100k records): < 300ms (with indexes < 60ms) NULL status check: < 200ms (with indexes < 40ms) Dashboard load (all sites): < 1s (with indexes < 300ms)
Troubleshooting: ================ If queries are still slow after index creation: 1. Check that statistics are up-to-date: ANALYZE TABLE 2. Verify indexes exist: SELECT * FROM user_indexes WHERE table_name = 'SENDER_STAGE' 3. Check for table locks: SELECT * FROM v$lock WHERE id1 = (SELECT object_id ...) 4. Consider table partitioning if SENDER_STAGE exceeds 100M rows
